A||||11||Black88GT||Multi-Amp install questions (stereo experts)||||5||
Z||000000||Black88GT||07-02-2001||09:11 PM||||Well, I finally got my amps in.  I built a rack for them today for behind the pass. seat, carpeted it with beechwood carpeting, the kind behind the seats at the top (not sure what its called).  But anyway, it looks nice.  My question is, what is the easiest way to install 2 amps?  I heard that I don't need 2 power cords, RCA cables, remote wires.  I want to do this as professional as possible.  I would imagine just cutting and splicing the power cord would not be right.  Any suggestions?  <P>Also, what sized fuse do you recommend for a 380 x 1 amp, the fuse near the battery that is.  I have a 50 watt but not sure if thats enough or too much.  <P>Where is the best ground for the amps in this location?<P>TIA<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff (AOL IM - FieroTT)<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">     <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I><BR><I>"The Customer Can Have Any Color He Wants So Long As It's Black" </I> -Henry Ford||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||07-02-2001||10:18 PM||||my amps are pretty small (2x15&2x30 4 channel and 2x30 2 channel) so i ran 8 guage front to back (amps in front trunk) to a small cap. then i split it at the positive terminal of the cap with eye lug terminals to each amp. the negatives the same only running to ground. 8 guage also.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DecadenceR||07-02-2001||10:42 PM||||A power distribution block may be a help to you as well. They aren't too expensive, and most stereo shops should have them. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||07-02-2001||11:10 PM||||<B>Power:</B> Use 8 gage wire and a 40 amp fuse at the battery. (the 2 amps have a max capability of 33 amps to draw. A 40 amp fuse will be fine. It will offer protection without current starving the power to the equipment) The amps should have their own fuses built-in already. Run the 8 gage wire to the inside of the car and insert a power distribution block. From the distribution block run 2 10 gage wires. 1 to each amp. Remember to use 10 gage for the ground wires and hook them to the same point.<BR> <B>(The rear bolt of the seat rail works great!)</B><BR>The 40 amp fuse and the large ground wires tied to the same point will aid in keeping engine noise (alternator whine) out of the system.<P><B>RCA'S:</B> This depends on the radio or how you are hooking up the system. Since one amp is a 380 X 1 I would assume it's for a subwoofer. Usually the RCA's from the radio go into a crossover which then splits to the designated amps. If you are using the internal crossover already in the amp (If equipped) you can run 1 RCA pair to the back and split with a high quality set of "Y" cables.<P><B>Remote:</B> The remote line for turn-on of accessory equipment (Amps, Crossovers,etc) can put out 250ma of power. This is enough to switch on 4 items. If you are hooking up just 2 amps, you can run 1 wire to the first amp and run a second wire from the first to the second amp. If you have more than 4 items hooked up (Example say 3 amps, a crossover, an Equalizer, and a digital processor) You need to run a seperate power line (16 gage) from the power distribution block to a relay. The control from the relay comes from the radio and the output of the relay goes to the equipment.<BR>If you try to run too much off the remote line you could burn out the driver inside the radio and nothing will turn on.<P>Hope the above helps.<P><BR>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||07-03-2001||11:28 AM||||I think that pretty much sums it up Oreif! Thanks a million.  I just need to find out who sells these power distribution blocks, shouldn't be a problem.<P>thanks again.||||reg||7
Z||000005||Black88GT||07-03-2001||11:53 AM||||Oh wait, about the RCA's.  I am using a Sony xplod 404-EQX and a sony xplod 1505SX.  The 404 is for the speakers, 40x4 with built in EQ, and 1505 is for the sub.   <P>I am not sure about the crossovers.  For the 404 amp it says "preamp and speaker-level inputs" <P>Oh crap.  I just noticed something.  They sent me the wrong amp.  I have the 1505 when I am supposed to have the 1502.  The 1505 is a 5 channel amp and the 1502 is a 2 channel amp.  What the heck am I supposed to do with a 5 channel amp AND a 4 channel amp.  Geesh.<BR>||||reg||1
